        detail: "Singapore hosts approximately 1.1 million low-wage migrant workers (mainly from Bangladesh, India, and China) who build its infrastructure and provide domestic and service labour. The COVID-19 pandemic exposed their living conditions: over 90% of Singapore's COVID cases in 2020 were among migrant workers housed in crowded dormitories (up to 20 per room). In response, the government introduced new dormitory standards, the Migrant Workers' Assistance Fund, and the Primary Care Plan for medical coverage. In the Gulf, the 'kafala' sponsorship system ties workers' legal status to their employers, creating conditions of exploitation; Qatar's 2022 World Cup construction drew global scrutiny after reports of thousands of migrant worker deaths (The Guardian estimated 6,500 from South Asian countries between 2010–2021, though attribution is disputed). Qatar subsequently abolished key elements of kafala and introduced a minimum wage. The fundamental tension: migrant labour underpins the prosperity of wealthy economies, yet migrant workers often lack the rights and protections afforded to citizens.",
