        detail: "The US Surgeon General's 2023 advisory declared social media poses 'a profound risk of harm' to youth mental health. Instagram's own leaked internal research (2021) showed 32% of teen girls said the platform made body image issues worse. In 2024, Australia became the first country to ban social media for children under 16. Singapore's approach has been more measured: IMDA's Code of Practice for Online Safety (2023) requires platforms to implement age-verification and parental controls, and MOE launched the 'Digital Wellness for Students' programme. Singapore's Institute of Mental Health reported a 50% increase in youth mental health consultations between 2019 and 2023. The National Youth Mental Health Survey (2024) found 1 in 3 young Singaporeans reported moderate-to-high stress levels, with social media comparison and academic pressure as the top two cited causes.",
        use: "FOR technology having unintended social costs: the platform's own research confirmed harm, yet Instagram chose not to act — corporate self-regulation fails when profit incentives conflict with user welfare. FOR government regulation being necessary: Australia's age ban and Singapore's IMDA codes show governments are stepping in where platforms won't self-regulate. FOR the interaction between technology and existing pressures: in Singapore, social media amplifies the already-intense academic pressure culture — the two causes are synergistic, not independent. COUNTER: correlation between social media use and mental health decline does not prove causation — pre-existing vulnerable individuals may self-select into heavy social media use. COUNTER: banning social media for under-16s (Australia) is paternalistic and practically unenforceable — determined teens will circumvent age verification. COUNTER: social media also provides community for marginalised youth (LGBTQ+, isolated, neurodiverse) who may not find acceptance offline — blanket restrictions harm those who benefit most.",
