        detail: "The US Surgeon General declared loneliness a public health crisis in 2023, noting its health effects equal to smoking 15 cigarettes a day. The UK appointed the world's first Minister for Loneliness in 2018 after a review found 9 million Britons regularly felt lonely. In Singapore, a 2024 survey by the National Volunteer and Philanthropy Centre found 47% of respondents felt lonely at least once a week, with young adults (18–35) reporting higher rates than the elderly — inverting the stereotype that loneliness is an old-age problem. Social media use is positively correlated with reported loneliness. Structural factors include: smaller household sizes (average Singapore household size fell from 4.0 in 1990 to 3.1 in 2024), long working hours (Singapore ranks 4th globally in average hours worked), the atomisation of urban living, and the shift from place-based communities (kampung, neighbourhood) to network-based relationships. Japan and South Korea have also appointed loneliness ministers.",
        use: "FOR loneliness as a policy problem requiring structural solutions: individual resilience messaging cannot address a phenomenon caused by structural forces — shrinking households, urban design, work culture, and social media. FOR the generational inversion: young adults being lonelier than elderly challenges the narrative that loneliness is an ageing problem — it is a structural problem of modern social organisation across all ages. FOR the technology paradox: social media promises connection but delivers comparison, performance, and passive consumption — the platforms designed to connect people are associated with greater loneliness. COUNTER: government intervention in social connection risks patronising citizens and medicalising ordinary human experience — not all solitude is loneliness, and not all loneliness requires state intervention. COUNTER: the loneliness epidemic may reflect measurement bias — social researchers who study loneliness find it; surveys may conflate momentary loneliness with chronic isolation. COUNTER: community organisations, religious institutions, and kampung-style neighbourhood planning are more effective responses than government policy — the state is not well-positioned to engineer social connection.",
