        use: "FOR the limits of globalisation: COVID-19 demonstrated that efficiency-optimised, just-in-time global supply chains are fragile — the same interconnection that enables prosperity creates systemic vulnerability. FOR strategic industrial policy: the CHIPS Act and Singapore's semiconductor expansion show governments reasserting control over strategically important industries after decades of market-led globalisation. FOR Singapore's strategic positioning: its role in semiconductor manufacturing gives it geopolitical leverage disproportionate to its size. COUNTER: 'friend-shoring' and reshoring are economically inefficient — duplicating supply chains raises costs for consumers, particularly in developing countries. COUNTER: supply chain nationalism risks fragmenting the global economy into competing blocs, reducing the gains from trade that have lifted billions out of poverty. COUNTER: Singapore's trade-to-GDP ratio of 300%+ means any deglobalisation trend is an existential economic threat, not an opportunity.",
