        region: "Singapore / Global", year: "2022–present",
        detail: "Singapore introduced mandatory Nutri-Grade labels (A–D) for pre-packaged drinks in December 2022, alongside a ban on advertising grade D (highest sugar/saturated fat) beverages — the first such policy in Asia. The proportion of lower-sugar (A/B grade) beverages available in supermarkets rose from 43% (2021) to 65% (2024) as manufacturers reformulated to avoid the stigma of grade D labels. The policy uses 'choice architecture' (nudge theory) rather than a direct tax or prohibition, preserving consumer choice while reshaping the default offering. The UK's sugar levy (2018) — a tiered tax on soft drinks — reduced sugar content in taxed beverages by 43.7% within 4 years and generated £380 million for sports and health programmes. Mexico's 'sin tax' on sugary drinks (2014) reduced consumption by 7.6% among the poorest urban households. Singapore's obesity rate has risen from 8.6% (2004) to 11.6% (2022) despite multiple healthy lifestyle campaigns, suggesting that voluntary awareness approaches are insufficient.",
        use: "FOR evidence that policy design matters more than coercion: Nutri-Grade achieved reformulation without banning or taxing — manufacturers responded to market reputational incentives, showing that 'nudge' policy can produce significant behaviour change without restricting freedom. FOR the sugar tax as proven intervention: the UK and Mexico data demonstrates that fiscal disincentives reduce consumption, particularly among lower-income groups who are most price-sensitive — taxes can redistribute health burdens. FOR state responsibility in structuring choice environments: when the food industry's profit motive is to maximise sugar content (because sugar is addictive and cheap), government has a legitimate role in counter-balancing commercial influences on individual choice. COUNTER: nutrition labels primarily influence the health-conscious middle class; those at greatest health risk (low-income, low-education) are least likely to use nutritional information in purchasing decisions. COUNTER: reformulation to meet grade A/B criteria may substitute sugar with artificial sweeteners whose long-term health effects are still being studied — solving one problem while potentially creating another. COUNTER: paternalistic food policies assume the state is better positioned than individuals to determine dietary choices — this logic, extended, could justify banning any food deemed unhealthy.",
